   "status": "ready",
   "date": "2026-10-02",
   "time": "10:00",
   "short": "What walks out the door",
   "idea": "N7: when your best data engineer leaves, the most valuable thing can leave with them",
   "format": "Single image · hand-drawn iceberg",
   "sample": {
    "id": "HD50",
    "creator": "Harry Dry (@marketingharry)",
    "what": "Hand-drawn iceberg: small 'what people think' tip, dense grid of rounded bubbles below the waterline, labels with arrows, closing banner",
    "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7459499258191237120"
   },
   "copy_basis": "Prompt doc: Viral Quotes (empowerment) template: hook, pattern interrupt, core message, misconception list, bridge, pain points, lessons, closing truth. HD50's own copy is a one-liner, too thin for Richard's point.",
   "cta": false,
   "media": {
    "type": "image",
    "src": "R4",
    "tall": false,
    "files": [
     "media/R4/image.jpg?v=202610121827"
    ],
    "download": {
     "href": "media/R4/BigHammer-b2-R4-2026-10-02-richard.png?v=202610121827",
     "name": "BigHammer-b2-R4-2026-10-02-richard.png",
     "ext": "png",
     "bytes": 1483743,
     "pages": 1,
     "w": 1080,
     "h": 1350
    }
   },
   "text": "When a senior data engineer hands in their notice, most companies worry about the code.\n\nHere's the thing.\n\nThe code is the easy part.\n\nIt's still there on Monday. What leaves is everything the code means:\n\n• Why that join exists\n• Which report the CFO actually trusts\n• What \"active customer\" means in this company\n• The workaround that keeps the 2am job alive\n• Who to call when a source system changes\n\nNone of that is written down. It lives in one person's head, and in pipeline logic that only they can read.\n\nI spent years selling data integration tools. I watched this happen at customer after customer. A good engineer left, and a quarter of \"why does this number look wrong?\" followed.\n\nThe fix isn't to stop people leaving. People should grow and move on.\n\nThe fix is where the knowledge lives.\n\nBusiness rules belong in your own repository, in plain view, where the next person can read them and the business can own them.\n\nYour engineers will always move on.\nYour logic shouldn't have to.\n\n#DataEngineering #DataLeadership #DataGovernance",
   "first_comment": "",
   "sources": [
    "Richard Lawrence 'general overview' doc: customer knowledge and logic kept in the customer's GitHub repository",
    "Richard Lawrence 'switch to enterprise' doc: career selling data integration (Talend, Pentaho, StreamSets)"
   ],
   "numbers_for_signoff": [],
   "batch": "2",
   "key": "b2-R4"
